## Does history support the positioning?

The test uses three years of daily MAS benchmark yields ({{ H.start }} to {{ H.end }}; {{ H.days }} days; days with a missing tenor are dropped). Each position is rebuilt on every start day from that day's curve, using today's sizing. It is held for six months, then revalued in full on the curve at the end date. Returns are active, in bp of fund value, and include carry and roll-down; maximum drawdown uses weekly marks. The {{ HD.n }} daily start dates give overlapping windows, which amount to about five independent six-month periods, so the hit rates are indicative only.

![SGS yields, 5s30s against its 3-year range, and rolling 6M active returns of the positions](report/figures/history.png)

{{ t.history }}

- **Duration underweight: history weakens it.** The SGS 10Y at {{ "%.2f"|format(HL.sgs10y_pct.now) }}% is {{ "%.2f"|format(-HL.sgs10y_pct.z) }} sd below its 3-year mean (lower than on {{ "%.0f"|format(100 - HL.sgs10y_pct.pct_rank) }}% of days) and close to its 12-month average of {{ "%.2f"|format(HL.sgs10y_pct.mean_12m) }}%. Over the window a {{ "%+.2f"|format(A.duration) }} yr underweight lost {{ "%.1f"|format(-HD.mean_bp) }} bp per six months on average and was right {{ "%.0f"|format(HD.hit_rate*100) }}% of the time, because the 10Y fell from a {{ "%.2f"|format(HL.sgs10y_pct.max) }}% high to a {{ "%.2f"|format(HL.sgs10y_pct.min) }}% low. The underweight did pay when the 10Y started at or below today's level ({{ "%.0f"|format(HD.like_today.hit_rate*100) }}% hit rate, {{ "%+.1f"|format(HD.like_today.mean_bp) }} bp average), but all of those starts fall in one episode ({{ HD.like_today.first|mon }}–{{ HD.like_today.last|mon }}), ahead of the 2026 sell-off. The underweight is therefore no longer presented as a source of return. It stays as a small hedge for the long-end view, with conviction cut from Medium to Low; the size is unchanged pending review.
- **5s30s steepener: mild support.** At {{ "%+.0f"|format(HL.s5s30_bp.now) }} bp, 5s30s is at its 3-year mean (z {{ "%+.2f"|format(HL.s5s30_bp.z) }}). That makes it flat only against the 12-month average of {{ "%+.0f"|format(HL.s5s30_bp.mean_12m) }} bp, not against the full three years. A similar steepener earned {{ "%+.1f"|format(HS.mean_bp) }} bp per six months on average with a {{ "%.0f"|format(HS.hit_rate*100) }}% hit rate. Started from a slope at or below today's, it had a {{ "%.0f"|format(HS.like_today.hit_rate*100) }}% hit rate and averaged {{ "%+.1f"|format(HS.like_today.mean_bp) }} bp, but all such starts fall in {{ HS.like_today.first|mon }}–{{ HS.like_today.last|mon }}, before and during the 2025 steepening. The {{ "%+.0f"|format(H.s5s30_6m_change.target_move_bp) }} bp target move happened in {{ "%.0f"|format(H.s5s30_6m_change.p_target*100) }}% of six-month windows, and a {{ "%.0f"|format(-H.s5s30_6m_change.stop_move_bp) }} bp flattening to the stop in {{ "%.0f"|format(H.s5s30_6m_change.p_stop*100) }}%. That is not a high-conviction setup, so conviction is cut from High to Medium. 10s30s at {{ "%+.0f"|format(HL.s10s30_bp.now) }} bp is also in line with its 3-year mean ({{ "%+.0f"|format(HL.s10s30_bp.mean) }} bp): an unrewarded long end has been the norm over the period, not an anomaly.
- **Rates book as a whole: no historical edge.** Taking every rates exposure in the portfolio together (with credit lines treated as SGS of the same maturity), the book averaged {{ "%+.1f"|format(HR.mean_bp) }} bp per six months, with a {{ "%.0f"|format(HR.hit_rate*100) }}% hit rate and a worst window of {{ "%+.0f"|format(HR.worst_bp) }} bp. On this evidence, the portfolio's excess return has to come from credit carry.
- **Credit is not testable.** MAS publishes no SGD credit spreads and there is no free daily SGD IG spread history, so the credit overweight still rests on the HDB comparable and the stated assumptions.
- **The risk model is, if anything, conservative.** Realised annualised volatility of daily yield changes was {{ "%.0f"|format(H.realised_vol_bp.values()|min) }}–{{ "%.0f"|format(H.realised_vol_bp.values()|max) }} bp by tenor, against assumed vols of 55–72 bp. At the long end the assumption is above realised (30Y: {{ "%.0f"|format(H.realised_vol_bp[30]) }} bp realised vs {{ "%.0f"|format(H.assumed_vol_bp[30]) }} bp assumed), so ex-ante TE is not understated.

## Method

**Curve.** SGS par yields are linearly interpolated onto a semi-annual grid. Discount factors are bootstrapped from par bonds:

$$DF_n=\frac{1-\tfrac{c_n}{2}\sum_{i<n}DF_i}{1+\tfrac{c_n}{2}},\qquad z(t)=2\left(DF(t)^{-1/(2t)}-1\right)$$

**Pricing.** Each bond is priced off the zero curve plus a constant z-spread $s$. Prices are dirty unless stated otherwise:

$$P=\sum_i CF_i\left(1+\frac{z(t_i)+s}{2}\right)^{-2t_i}$$

**Risk.** All measures are effective, computed by central-difference revaluation with $\Delta y=1$ bp. Shocks are applied to the par curve, which is then re-bootstrapped.

$$D=\frac{P_{-}-P_{+}}{2P_0\,\Delta y},\quad \mathrm{DV01}=D\cdot MV\cdot10^{-4},\quad KRD_k=\frac{P(-\delta_k)-P(+\delta_k)}{2P_0\,\Delta y},\quad \sum_k KRD_k=D$$

Here $\delta_k$ is a triangular 1 bp bump centred on key tenor $k\in\{2,5,10,15,20,30\}$, held flat beyond the end keys. Spread duration $SD$ uses the same formula with $s$ bumped instead of the curve.

**Conventions.** Yields, zeros and z-spreads are semi-annual bond-equivalent, with coupons paid semi-annually and cash flows on an exact half-year grid back-stepped from maturity (no day count or holiday calendar). DV01 is reported as a positive number for a long-duration position: it is the S\$ loss for a 1 bp parallel rise in par yields. Portfolio yield is the market-value-weighted average of bond yields to maturity.

**Benchmark weights.** Weights are set so that each line contributes equal duration:

$$w_i=\frac{1/D_i}{\sum_j 1/D_j}$$

**Tracking error.** Let $x$ be the active exposures (6 key-rate durations and 3 sector spread durations) and $\Sigma$ the annualised factor covariance:

$$TE=\sqrt{x^\top\Sigma\,x+\sum_{i\in\text{credit}}\left(w_i\,SD_i\,\sigma^{\text{idio}}_i\right)^2},\qquad \Sigma_{ij}=\sigma_i\sigma_j\rho_{ij},\quad \rho^{\text{rates}}_{ij}=e^{-\lambda|T_i-T_j|}$$

**Return attribution** over horizon $h$:

$$R_h=\underbrace{y\,h}_{\text{carry}}+\underbrace{\left(R^{\text{static}}_h-y\,h\right)}_{\text{roll-down}}\;\underbrace{-\tilde D\,\overline{\Delta y}}_{\text{duration}}\;\underbrace{-\sum_k \widetilde{KRD}_k\left(\Delta y_k-\overline{\Delta y}\right)}_{\text{curve}}\;\underbrace{-\widetilde{SD}\,\Delta s}_{\text{spread}}\;+\;\varepsilon$$

- $y$ is yield to maturity.
- $R^{\text{static}}_h$ is the full-revaluation return on an unchanged curve.
- $\overline{\Delta y}$ is the mean of the six key-rate shocks.
- Tildes mark risk measured on the bond aged to $h$, scaled by $P_h/P_0$.
- $\varepsilon$ is full revaluation minus the linear terms (convexity and cross terms).

Active return is $R^A=\sum_i w^P_iR_i-\sum_j w^B_jR_j$.

