**Assumptions.**

- **Benchmark weighting: duration-weighted** (equal rate risk per tenor, in the spirit of a liability-style benchmark) rather than market-value weighted, so that no single tenor dominates rate risk and the benchmark does not depend on line-by-line SGS outstanding amounts.
- Benchmark lines are **par bonds** at the MAS benchmark yields (coupon = yield), not the actual SGS coupons and maturities.
- Fund size of **S\$500m**, used only to express DV01 in S\$.
- Tracking-error budget of **100 bp p.a.**
- Rate vols of 55–72 bp p.a. by tenor, decay $\lambda$ = 0.03. Sector spread vols of 10/30/45 bp, issuer-specific vols of 5/15/30 bp, spread–spread correlation 0.7 and spread–rate correlation −0.2.
- The scenario horizon is **6 months**. Coupons are received but not reinvested, and curve shocks are judgmental (see `src/sgdfi/scenarios.py`).
- HDB 2031 maturity is approximated as 4.56 years from 30 Oct 2026.
- Coupons on illustrative credit bonds are set so each prices near par at its spread, rounded to 1/80%.

## How to reproduce

Requires Python 3.10+.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/HuwaWei/sgd-fixed-income-portfolio.git
cd sgd-fixed-income-portfolio
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
make test analysis
```

`make test` runs the unit tests. `make analysis` (equivalently `PYTHONPATH=src python -m sgdfi`) reads `data/`, rebuilds `output/*.md`, `output/results.json`, the figures, the commentary and this README. It needs no network: the yield history is committed in `data/`, and `python scripts/fetch_mas_history.py 2023-10-30 2026-10-30` re-downloads it from MAS. `make pdf` renders the 2-page PDF and additionally needs `pandoc` and Node with Playwright/Chromium; `make all` runs install, tests, analysis and PDF in one step.

## Limitations

- **Single-date snapshot.** All positioning analytics are as at {{ r.as_of }}. Attribution is ex-ante (scenario-based), and the history test is a simulated backtest; neither is a realised performance record.
- **Credit spreads are mostly illustrative.** There is no public SGD IG spread curve for 30 Oct 2026. Statutory-board spreads rest on a single comparable (HDB's September 4Y print, whose implied spread ranged {{ r.hdb_comparable.implied_spread_bp[0] }}–{{ r.hdb_comparable.implied_spread_bp[1] }} bp as SGS moved); at the prior {{ r.spread_sensitivity[0].hdb_bp }}/{{ r.spread_sensitivity[0].sb10_bp }} bp the pick-up is {{ "%+.0f"|format(r.spread_sensitivity[0].pickup_bp) }} bp, and at the top of the range {{ "%+.0f"|format(r.spread_sensitivity[2].pickup_bp) }} bp. Bank, GLC and REIT spreads (55–125 bp) are assumptions, and those lines are generic proxies, not specific issues. There is no liquidity, bid/offer or new-issue concession modelling, and spreads are constant across tenor.
- **S\$NEER slope.** MAS does not publish the slope; ~1.25% p.a. is a market estimate.
- **Short market history.** The history test covers three years of SGS yields, which is essentially one rally followed by one sell-off; its overlapping six-month windows amount to about five independent observations. There is no SGD credit-spread or SORA history. Tracking error and scenarios still depend on assumed vols and judgmental shocks rather than an empirical covariance matrix.
- **Benchmark is a model construct**, not a published index (e.g. FTSE/iBoxx). The duration weighting makes it front-end heavy by market value (about half in 2Y) and shorter than a market-value-weighted SGS index, so active positions are measured against equal risk per tenor.
- **Simplified conventions.** Cash flows sit on an exact half-year grid with no day count or holiday calendar, and the 6M and 1Y T-bill yields are treated as semi-annual par yields.
- There are no transaction costs, repo/funding, FX hedging or SORA/IRS overlays. In practice the curve trade could be run more efficiently in SGD IRS.

